# The `cn=config` half of the fixture (issue #142): what `cn=svc` may READ, and how much of it.
#
# Applied with ldapmodify over ldapi:/// as EXTERNAL after the server is up, because the
# bootstrap directory the image reads holds DATA only -- these are server configuration.

# THE SERVICE ACCOUNT MUST BE ABLE TO READ THE TREE. The image's default ACL ends with
# `to * by self read by dn="cn=admin,..." write by * none`, so a bound `cn=svc` can read its own
# entry and nothing else. OpenLDAP reports a denied read as `noSuchObject` (32) rather than as a
# permission error -- back-mdb sets that result when the requester lacks ACL_DISCLOSE -- so a
# tester reading ldapsearch output sees "No such object" for a subtree that plainly exists.
#
# MEASURED, by deleting this ACL from a live server and running both suites:
#
#   * `ldap_live` goes 6 passed / 1 failed. Only the PAGING test binds as `cn=svc` (the others
#     bind as `cn=admin`, which the default ACL already allows), and it fails on its own fixture
#     precondition -- "the fixture must refuse an unpaged read with sizeLimitExceeded" -- with
#     rc=32, because a denied read and a refused-for-size read are different results.
#   * `ldap_live_pass` goes 0 passed / 1 failed, on "the connector produced no plan": the sweep
#     binds as `cn=svc` exactly as production does, and `search_all` ends in `result.success()?`
#     so rc=32 becomes a `DirectoryError` rather than an empty entry list.
#
# Two earlier versions of this comment were wrong about that. The first said the directory would
# "look EMPTY" and the diff would refuse it as everybody-vanished; nothing gets far enough to
# diff anything. The second said every live test would fail; six of them bind as admin and do
# not care.
#
# Inserted BEFORE the default catch-all: olcAccess is ordered and the first match wins.
dn: olcDatabase={1}mdb,cn=config
changetype: modify
add: olcAccess
olcAccess: {2}to dn.subtree="dc=example,dc=test" by dn.exact="cn=svc,dc=example,dc=test" read by * break

# THE SEARCH LIMITS that make the paging test able to fail.
#
# size.soft=2 with five people under ou=People means an UNPAGED search is refused with
# `sizeLimitExceeded` (LDAP result 4), while size.prtotal=unlimited lets a PAGED one walk the
# whole set. Without the asymmetry a client that never sends the RFC 2696 control would pass the
# paging test, which is the defect that test exists to catch.
dn: olcDatabase={1}mdb,cn=config
changetype: modify
add: olcLimits
olcLimits: dn.exact="cn=svc,dc=example,dc=test" size.soft=2 size.hard=2 size.prtotal=unlimited size.pr=unlimited
